This builds on, and owes most of what works to:

felucca-bsp (bsp/, a git submodule; MPL-2.0)
  https://github.com/hugelton/felucca-bsp
  Leo Kuroshita (@kurogedelic), Hügelton Instruments; Charles Vestal; Kerem Kilic (keremimo); and the
  contributors named in its own NOTICE. The chip layer, USB device, interrupts, flash, boot guard and
  console here are theirs. runtime/gx_runtime.c, runtime/app.ld and build.sh follow its
  examples/hwtest.

jl-uboot-tool (tools/jl-uboot-tool, a git submodule; MIT)
  https://github.com/kagaimiq/jl-uboot-tool
  Copyright (c) 2023 Andrey Grigoryev (kagaimiq). The ROM bootloader protocol, its USB loader and
  JieLi's ciphers. kagaimiq's notes (https://github.com/kagaimiq/jielie) map much of this chip family.

MvaveFM1Unbricker (MIT)
  https://github.com/Quixotic7/MvaveFM1Unbricker
  Copyright (c) 2026 Michael P Jones. tools/gx_flash.py's loader upload follows its fm1_unbrick.py.

M-VAVE FM1 Open SDK packaging (MIT)
  https://github.com/m-vave/fm1_open, sdk/Package.cs
  Copyright (c) 2023 Andrey Grigoryev (kagaimiq), 2026 M-VAVE. tools/jl_package.py follows its
  flash-image layout and records.

FM-1-RE (WTFPL)
  https://github.com/AL-255/FM-1-RE
  The reverse engineering of the FM-1, the sister device this work started from.

The Galaxia's pin map and audio set-up were worked out on a real pedal, from its behaviour and from
the register sequences its stock firmware uses. No M-VAVE or JieLi code or firmware is included.

